<%#
    The console's view switcher, at the TOP of every console page and STICKY.

    WHY IT IS A PARTIAL RATHER THAN THREE COPIES. Three views need this block, and two
    of them already carried a hand-written copy that had DRIFTED: the answers view emitted
    `class=""` where the roadmap omitted the attribute. That is the small kind of
    difference that survives a review. A partial cannot drift, and the ORDER below is the
    point, so it is written once.

    WHY IT MOVED TO THE TOP. It used to sit after the heading and the switchers, so on a
    390px-wide phone the reader had to scroll past a two-line `<h1>` and any banner before
    reaching the only way to change page. The tabs were present and effectively invisible,
    which reads as a missing feature rather than a layout choice. Measured in a browser
    before and after: the tab row is now the first element below the banner band.

    WHY STICKY. The roadmap is a long page. A reader halfway down it has no way to reach
    the answers view without scrolling back, which is the same problem one screen lower.
    `position: sticky` keeps it against the top of the viewport while the content moves,
    so the tabs are reachable wherever the reader is.

    STICKY NEEDS AN OPAQUE BACKGROUND. Scrolling content passes underneath, so a
    transparent bar would show the text through it.

    THE ACTIVE PAGE IS MARKED WITH `aria-current`, not only with a class: a screen reader
    announces "current page" from it, and colour alone does not say which page you are on.
%>
<% if (typeof activeView !== 'undefined' && activeView) { %>
    <nav class="views" aria-label="Console views">
        <a href="<%= basePath %>/admin"<%= activeView === 'console' ? ' class="active" aria-current="page"' : '' %>>Console</a>
        <a href="<%= basePath %>/admin/answers"<%= activeView === 'answers' ? ' class="active" aria-current="page"' : '' %>>Every answer</a>
        <a href="<%= basePath %>/admin/roadmap"<%= activeView === 'roadmap' ? ' class="active" aria-current="page"' : '' %>>Roadmap</a>
    </nav>
<% } %>
